    "top_frame": "Seven runs on fourteen hits, and they never trailed.",
    "what_this_means": "Cole gave them five-plus innings of clean baseball. The lineup broke through early. Three in the first, two more in the eighth. And the bullpen sealed it. They didn't need drama. They didn't need to fight back. They took the lead and held it from wire to wire, which is what good teams do against inferior competition on the road. Jazz Chisholm Jr. drove in two runs and kept the line moving. Cody Bellinger found his swing. The offense wasn't flashy. It was complete. Headrick, Blackburn, and Schreiber combined for 3.2 scoreless innings after Cole left. This is what efficient looks like: control the game early, finish clean, go home.",
    "what_to_watch": "The margin is growing where it matters. They're now 6-4 over their last ten games, and the trend has flipped from fragile to stabilizing. They converted runners on base when it counted. Four times in high-leverage spots. Which is the difference between a night that feels tight and one that feels settled. The bullpen didn't just survive; it locked the door. Schreiber got the final out without needing anyone else to warm up. That's the threshold: when a team stops having to grind and starts having to execute, execution starts to feel routine. They've beaten this problem before.",
